<PubmedArticleSet><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">29526696</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2019</Year><Month>01</Month><Day>07</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2022</Year><Month>07</Month><Day>16</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1097-4164</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>69</Volume><Issue>6</Issue><PubDate><Year>2018</Year><Month>Mar</Month><Day>15</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Molecular cell</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Mol Cell</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>LKB1, Salt-Inducible Kinases, and MEF2C Are Linked Dependencies in Acute Myeloid Leukemia.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>1017</StartPage><EndPage>1027.e6</EndPage><MedlinePgn>1017-1027.e6</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.molcel.2018.02.011</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1097-2765(18)30109-6</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>The lineage-specific transcription factor (TF) MEF2C is often deregulated in leukemia. However, strategies to target this TF have yet to be identified. Here, we used a domain-focused CRISPR screen to reveal an essential role for LKB1 and its Salt-Inducible Kinase effectors (SIK3, in a partially redundant manner with SIK2) to maintain MEF2C function in acute myeloid leukemia (AML). A key phosphorylation substrate of SIK3 in this context is HDAC4, a repressive cofactor of MEF2C. Consequently, targeting of&#160;LKB1 or SIK3 diminishes histone acetylation at MEF2C-bound enhancers and deprives leukemia cells of the output of this essential TF. We also found that MEF2C-dependent leukemias are sensitive to&#160;on-target chemical inhibition of SIK activity. This study reveals a chemical strategy to block MEF2C function in AML, highlighting how an oncogenic TF can be disabled by targeting of upstream kinases.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>Copyright &#169; 2018 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Tarumoto</LastName><ForeName>Yusuke</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory, Cold Spring Harbor, NY 11724, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Lu</LastName><ForeName>Bin</ForeName><Initials>B</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory, Cold Spring Harbor, NY 11724, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Somerville</LastName><ForeName>Tim D D</ForeName><Initials>TDD</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory, Cold Spring Harbor, NY 11724, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Huang</LastName><ForeName>Yu-Han</ForeName><Initials>YH</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory, Cold Spring Harbor, NY 11724, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Milazzo</LastName><ForeName>Joseph P</ForeName><Initials>JP</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory, Cold Spring Harbor, NY 11724, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Wu</LastName><ForeName>Xiaoli S</ForeName><Initials>XS</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory, Cold Spring Harbor, NY 11724, USA; Genetics Program, Stony Brook University, Stony Brook, NY 11794, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Klingbeil</LastName><ForeName>Olaf</ForeName><Initials>O</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory, Cold Spring Harbor, NY 11724, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>El Demerdash</LastName><ForeName>Osama</ForeName><Initials>O</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory, Cold Spring Harbor, NY 11724, USA.</Affilia